<ArticleLayout
  title="Dishwasher Sizes Australia: Measure Before You Buy"
  seoTitle="Dishwasher Sizes Australia: 60 cm, 45 cm and Cavity Guide"
  description="Compare Australian dishwasher formats and measure width, height, depth, services, kickboards and open door clearance before buying."
  category="Kitchen"
  date="Updated September 2026"
  datePublished="2026-07-23"
  dateModified="2026-09-10"
  readingTime="10 minute read"
  faqs={faqs}
  showAffiliateDisclosure={false}
  image="/images/guides/dishwasher-sizes-australia.webp"
  imageAlt="An open stainless steel dishwasher installed below a kitchen bench showing racks, cabinetry and door clearance"
  imagePosition="center 62%"
>
  <p>
    Most Australian kitchens are planned around a nominal 600 mm dishwasher space. That is a useful
    starting point, not a guarantee. Full size bodies are commonly just under or at 600 mm wide,
    while height, feet, hoses and open door measurements differ.
  </p>
  <p>
    Measure the finished opening before comparing programs. A quiet machine with flexible racks is
    still the wrong purchase if its feet cannot level within the height, the hose is pinched behind
    it or the open door blocks the only kitchen walkway.
  </p>

  <MeasurementWorksheetLink appliance="dishwasher" />

  <section aria-labelledby="dishwasher-dimensions-heading">
    <h2 id="dishwasher-dimensions-heading">Dishwasher dimensions in Australia: quick cavity check</h2>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li><strong>Width:</strong> Compare the narrowest finished opening with the cavity width in the exact model's installation drawing. Keep overall width, chassis width and any projecting fascia or panel separate.</li>
      <li><strong>Height:</strong> Measure from the finished floor to the underside of the benchtop on both sides, then confirm the model's body height and adjustable-foot range.</li>
      <li><strong>Depth:</strong> Allow for the appliance body, projecting parts, hoses and the installation arrangement specified by the manufacturer.</li>
      <li><strong>Services:</strong> Confirm where the inlet hose, drain hose and power lead must run, their available lengths and whether connections belong in a neighbouring cabinet.</li>
      <li><strong>Door clearance:</strong> Mark the open door and extended lower rack, then leave enough room for a person to load it without blocking the main walkway.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Do not assume a new dishwasher will fit because the old one did. Use the chosen model's
      installation drawing and instructions before altering cabinetry, kickboards or services.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="dishwasher-types-heading">
    <h2 id="dishwasher-types-heading">Dishwasher formats at a glance</h2>
    <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
    <div class="comparison-wrap">
      <table class="comparison-table">
        <caption>Common dishwasher formats in Australian kitchens</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Format</th>
            <th scope="col">Common nominal width</th>
            <th scope="col">What changes</th>
            <th scope="col">When it may suit</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Freestanding</td>
            <td>600 mm</td>
            <td>Finished top and sides, product height and whether the top can be removed</td>
            <td>Simple replacement where the appliance remains visible</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Built under</td>
            <td>600 mm</td>
            <td>No finished worktop, lower body height, kickboard treatment and fixing method</td>
            <td>A neater fit below an existing benchtop without a cabinet door panel</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fully integrated</td>
            <td>600 mm</td>
            <td>Custom door panel, hinge geometry, panel weight and controls on the door edge</td>
            <td>Matching the dishwasher front to the cabinetry</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Slimline</td>
            <td>450 mm</td>
            <td>Smaller rack capacity and fewer place settings</td>
            <td>A narrow kitchen that cannot provide a full size opening</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Drawer dishwasher</td>
            <td>600 mm</td>
            <td>Single or double drawers, different height and plumbing layout</td>
            <td>Waist height access or separate small loads</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ApplianceMeasurementResource appliance="dishwasher" />

  <section aria-labelledby="current-dishwasher-examples-heading" data-appliance-model-examples="dishwasher">
    <h2 id="current-dishwasher-examples-heading">Three current models show why product size and cavity size differ</h2>
    <p>These Australian models were checked against current manufacturer listings and installation documents on <time datetime="2026-09-10">10 September 2026</time>. They illustrate different measuring problems; they are not recommendations or firsthand test results. All figures are in millimetres; three-number dimensions use <strong>width × height × depth</strong>.</p>
    <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
    <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Current dishwasher dimensions and installation requirements">
      <table class="comparison-table">
        <caption>Compare the appliance, required cavity and open-door space</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Current Australian model</th><th scope="col">Published product dimensions (mm)</th><th scope="col">What the installation documents add</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Bosch SMS6HCI02A<br />Freestanding</th><td>600 × 845 × 600 with the worktop fitted.<br />Total depth with the door open 90°: 1,155.</td><td>The specification sheet gives a maximum foot adjustment of 20. The product width alone is not a verified cavity requirement. Check the current manual's installation section and the particular underbench arrangement before altering cabinetry.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Fisher &amp; Paykel DD60SCX9<br />Single DishDrawer</th><td>599 × 410 × 573 overall.<br />Chassis depth: 553, with a 20 mm drawer panel making up the overall depth.</td><td>The March 2026 installation guide requires a cavity 600–610 wide, at least 412 high and at least 579 deep. Check the drawer panel clearances and the service layout in that guide as well as full drawer extension.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Miele G 5263 SCVi BK Active Plus<br />Fully integrated; product 11587640</th><td>598 × 805 × 550 for the appliance body, before the cabinetry panel.<br />Total depth with door open: 1,165.</td><td>The niche is 600 wide, 805–870 high and at least 550 deep. Use the standard-height assembly drawing, then check the custom panel, hinge movement and kickboard requirements separately.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A nominal “60 cm” label, an overall width and a required cavity width answer different questions. Use the manufacturer's cavity drawing to judge the opening, then check which parts sit inside it and which project beyond it. Do not add or subtract a standard gap from the headline product width.</p>
    <p>The DishDrawer example also shows why overall depth and chassis depth are not interchangeable: the drawer panel contributes to one but not the other. For a fully integrated dishwasher, the cabinetmaker's panel and its opening path are additional checks. Open-door depth does not include the space a person needs to stand and load the machine.</p>
    <h3>Manufacturer sources for these examples</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bosch SMS6HCI02A:</strong> <a href="https://www.bosch-home.com.au/en/product/dishwashers/freestanding-dishwashers/dishwasher-60-cm-wide/SMS6HCI02A" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Australian model details</a>; <a href="https://media3.bsh-group.com/Documents/specsheet/en-AU/SMS6HCI02A.pdf#page=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Australian specification sheet, pages 1–2</a>; <a href="https://media3.bsh-group.com/Documents/9002017246_A.pdf#page=10" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">manual: installation and connection, pages 10–11</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Fisher &amp; Paykel DD60SCX9:</strong> <a href="https://www.fisherpaykel.com/au/dishwashing/built-under/series-7-contemporary-single-dishdrawer-dishwasher-dd60scx9-82316.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Australian model details</a>; <a href="https://dam.fisherpaykel.com/KZ3PKN00/at/79xxgf3js7vvp9t95gwpm6q/FP-InstallGuide-en-DD60ST4-DD60ST4HNX9-DD60SDFHTX9-DD60SC-DD60SAX9-DishDrawer-0-431375C-NZ-AU-UK-IE-SG.pdf#page=4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">March 2026 installation guide, pages 4–6</a> for chassis, panel and cavity dimensions. Use the DD60SCX9 column rather than the taller adjacent models.</li>
      <li><strong>Miele G 5263 SCVi BK Active Plus:</strong> <a href="https://shop.miele.com.au/en/kitchen/dishwashers/fully-integrated-dishwashers/g-5263-scvi-bk-active-plus-fully-integrated-dishwasher-zid11587640/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Australian model and niche specifications</a>; <a href="https://media.miele.com/downloads/82/b0/00_C7F13838347D1EDFADF32AAD961B82B0.pdf#page=92" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">operating manual, page 92</a>; <a href="https://media.miele.com/downloads/40/16/00_C7F1383883351EDEB78F4E75869F4016.pdf#page=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">assembly plan, sheets 1–2</a>. The standard-height and XXL dimensions are different.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Before ordering, match the complete model code to the manufacturer's current documents and have the installer confirm the cabinet, door panel and services against that version.</p>
  </section>

  <h2>Measure the opening at the front and back</h2>
  <p>
    Record the width just inside the cabinet face and again near the rear. Measure at the floor,
    middle and underside of the bench. Use the smallest figure that reflects the actual path of the
    appliance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Repeat the height measurement on both sides. Tiles, floorboards and benchtops can slope. Include
    any trim below the benchtop and check whether the appliance feet can adjust within the finished
    height.
  </p>

  <h2>Depth includes hoses, the door and the person loading it</h2>
  <p>
    Product depth is only the beginning. Water and drain hoses need a route that does not crush or
    kink them. The plug and power point must be positioned as allowed by the installation guide.
    Some models use a neighbouring cabinet for services rather than placing connections directly
    behind the machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mark the open door on the floor. Then add the space needed to pull out the lower rack and stand
    in front with a plate. A door can clear the island by a few millimetres and still leave no usable
    loading space.
  </p>

  <h2>Freestanding, built under and integrated are not interchangeable labels</h2>
  <p>
    A freestanding model has a finished worktop and side panels. Some manufacturers permit removal
    of the top for installation under a bench, but others do not. A built under dishwasher is made
    to sit beneath the benchtop and normally keeps its own visible front.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fully integrated dishwasher takes a cabinetry panel. That panel has specified dimensions and
    weight, and its hinge movement must clear the kickboard. Confirm panel preparation before the
    cabinetmaker finishes the doors.
  </p>

  <h2>The kickboard can stop the door opening</h2>
  <p>
    Check the appliance drawing against the kickboard height and setback. Integrated doors move
    differently from standard freestanding doors, and a thick or forward kickboard can hit the panel.
    Do not cut finished cabinetry until the installation template and instructions for the chosen appliance
    are available.
  </p>

  <h2>Services need a safe, accessible route</h2>
  <p>
    Confirm the inlet hose, drain hose and power lead lengths against the actual connection points.
    Do not use an extension lead, improvise plumbing or place connections where the appliance body
    will crush them. Plumbing and electrical work should be handled by appropriately qualified
    people where required.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check whether the model accepts cold water only or a controlled hot water connection, along
    with its maximum inlet temperature and pressure requirements. The correct answer comes from the
    exact installation guide, not another dishwasher from the same brand.
  </p>

  <h2>Compare rack layout with your dishes</h2>
  <p>
    Place settings are useful for comparing capacity, but they do not describe a large chopping
    board, deep pasta bowl, long stem glass or the saucepan used every night. Take the diameter and
    height of awkward items to the showroom and test how the adjustable rack changes the lower space.
  </p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Check the largest dinner plate against the lower rack and spray arm.</li>
    <li>See whether bowls stand securely or collapse together.</li>
    <li>Compare a cutlery basket with a third tray for the utensils you own.</li>
    <li>Lower and raise the upper rack before judging tall glass clearance.</li>
    <li>Make sure handles and controls are comfortable for the main users.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Read water and energy labels together</h2>
  <p>
    The Australian Government <a href="https://www.waterrating.gov.au/consumers/water-rating-label" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Water Rating Label guide</a>{" "}
    explains that dishwasher labels show litres per wash, place settings and the cycle used for
    testing. Compare similar capacities and note that a more efficient test cycle may take longer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Australian Government's <a href="https://www.energyrating.gov.au/consumer-information/understand-energy-rating-label" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Energy Rating Label guide</a>{" "}
    explains the annual kilowatt-hour figure and its usage assumptions. Actual cost will differ
    with program choice, frequency, water heating and electricity tariff. Compare similar
    capacities; a strong star rating alone does not show whether the racks, cycle and size suit
    your household.
  </p>

  <h2>Dishwasher measurement checklist</h2>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Measure width and height at the front, middle and rear.</li>
    <li>Check floor level and the adjustment range of the feet.</li>
    <li>Record body depth, hose space and open door depth.</li>
    <li>Leave enough room to pull out the lower rack and stand in front.</li>
    <li>Confirm the exact service route and connection requirements.</li>
    <li>Check the kickboard and custom panel drawings for integrated models.</li>
    <li>Load your largest plates, bowls and glasses in a display model where possible.</li>
    <li>Compare WELS litres and energy use between similar capacities.</li>
  </ul>

  <RelatedGuidesBlock guides={relatedGuides} />
</ArticleLayout>
